WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.000
SIRUI SUN: Good
afternoon, everyone

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.105
and welcome to Best Practices
for GCE Enterprise Deployments.

00:00:13.105 --> 00:00:14.480
Thank you all so
much for coming.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.250
I know we're in the
homestretch of Next.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.902
So, I really appreciate
that you all could be here.

00:00:19.902 --> 00:00:20.610
My name is Sirui.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:23.220
I am a product manager on
the Compute Engine team,

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.960
and the goal for
the next 15 minutes

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.840
is going to be for me to share
with you all some of the best

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.380
practices and learnings that
we've picked up over the years,

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:35.010
and working both internally at
Google and with our customers

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:39.140
to deploy truly large
deployments of GC.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.805
And so we can have
two data sources,

00:00:41.805 --> 00:00:43.930
as part of sharing that
information with you today,

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:45.030
we have two data sources.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.430
The first one is the
learnings that we picked up

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:48.670
that I just talked about.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.090
And the second one
is going to be,

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:52.590
I want to share with you some
of the really exciting feature

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:55.260
announcements that
we've made here at Next,

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.690
as well as leading up to
Next so you have the latest

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.780
and greatest product details
to take with you as you go

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.523
and scale out your
GC Deployments.

00:01:03.523 --> 00:01:05.190
So, the first thing
I want to talk about

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:08.340
is, when we talk about an
effective G Suite Enterprise

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.650
Deployment what
is our North Star?

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.020
Like, what is the goal
that we're going towards?

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.830
And so, one part of the
goal is for sure agility.

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:20.160
Almost every customer I talk to,
they want to get to the Cloud

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:21.360
to move quickly.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.940
They want to use the scale
and the power of the Cloud

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.680
to quickly reach
their business goals

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.360
and to compete in the
space that they're in.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.740
But the problem here is,
agility can't be our only focus,

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.785
because if you're
just moving fast,

00:01:34.785 --> 00:01:36.660
you might be heading
straight towards a cliff

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:37.943
and not know it.

00:01:37.943 --> 00:01:39.860
And so the North Star I
want to introduce here

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:42.150
for this talk, the goal that
we want to reach towards,

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:45.220
is agility with the
right guardrails.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:48.060
So for example, you want to
know that you're moving quickly,

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:51.810
but you're doing so in
a cost-effective way

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:55.290
and also in a way that
satisfies your legal

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:58.650
and regulatory and
compliance requirements.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:00.600
You're going to want to
mix the both of those,

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.428
and this talk is going to
be about how you move fast.

00:02:03.428 --> 00:02:05.970
But it's also going to be about
how you use the tools that we

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:09.210
offer you in GCE, and
more broadly in GCP,

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:12.560
to achieve the right
guardrails as well.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.250
So the first thing I'll do is
I'll look inside our tool box

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:16.870
and talk a little
bit about the tools

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:20.710
that you have available in GCE
to do these sorts of things,

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:22.750
with kind of a focus
on the new features

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:24.640
that we launched here at Next.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:25.870
This is a 300-level talk.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:27.607
I'm going to assume
some prior knowledge.

00:02:27.607 --> 00:02:29.440
So we're going to focus
on some of the newer

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:31.400
and exciting things.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.120
The first section is going to
be about choosing the right VM

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.237
types.

00:02:35.237 --> 00:02:36.820
This is going to be
key in making sure

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:38.980
that your workloads run
quickly and effectively

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:41.460
on GCE, and also in
a cost efficient way.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.210
So the first thing I'm
going to talk about

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:45.080
is the different machine
types that we offer

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.205
and there have been some
exciting new announcements

00:02:47.205 --> 00:02:49.095
in this space

00:02:49.095 --> 00:02:50.470
Ever since GCE
has been launched,

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:52.450
we've had the general
purpose machine type.

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:55.660
This is the machine type that
you use kind of by default,

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:58.420
and we find that it has the
best price-performance ratios

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:00.280
in general and
that is well suited

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.020
for a lot of
different workloads.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:04.510
You should always start here
when assessing your workloads,

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:06.010
and we've seen them
effectively used

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:07.990
for all sorts of
different workloads,

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:11.260
including web serving,
databases, mobile gaming,

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:12.350
you name it.

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:15.460
So, you start here, but we
had this for a long time,

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.330
and we heard a lot
of customers say,

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:20.560
well, I have maybe a very
large in-memory database that

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:23.290
needs to store a ton
of data in that VM

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:26.650
for, say online
analytical processing.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.360
And so at a previous
Next, we announced

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:30.100
the memory-optimized
machine types.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:32.230
These, upon the
initial announcement,

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:35.800
these [? new ?] VM supported
up to 4 terabytes of memory.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:37.540
But in the time
since then, we've

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:39.880
heard from a lot of customers
that, well, my database

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:43.180
is constantly growing as the
data that I work with expands,

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:46.390
and so we want to make sure that
we have the VM shapes to keep

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:47.440
up with that.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.710
Here at this event,
we announced that we

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:53.290
will have memory-optimized
machines will extending

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:55.510
that line to have up to
12 terabytes of memory,

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:57.303
and that's coming soon.

00:03:57.303 --> 00:03:59.720
Another type of workload that
we hear from customers a lot

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:02.240
about, is they
have workloads that

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.250
are very compute-performance
intensive.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:08.720
And so in response
to that, we're

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.200
announcing here the
compute-optimized line

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:12.200
in machine shapes.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:15.380
So why would you want this
particular type of machines?

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:17.360
We have customers
that are really

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:19.140
sensitive to
real-time performance.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:22.598
So for example, if you're
running a AAA gaming server,

00:04:22.598 --> 00:04:24.140
every millisecond
counts and you want

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:27.630
to make sure you have the
fastest per core performance.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:30.200
Another reason you'd want this
is, we see some customers,

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:33.563
they have licensing agreements
where they license per core.

00:04:33.563 --> 00:04:35.480
And so they want to
squeeze the most juice out

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:36.930
of each core as possible.

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:40.100
So those are two reasons to
look into the compute-optimize

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:40.940
machine type.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:42.450
This is now in alpha.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:45.052
So if one of those
cases sounds like it'd

00:04:45.052 --> 00:04:46.760
be useful for your
workloads, I encourage

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:48.885
you to check out the blog
post that we have on this

00:04:48.885 --> 00:04:51.860
and you'll have a sign
up link for the alpha.

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:54.507
That's all I'll go into
for machine shapes,

00:04:54.507 --> 00:04:56.090
but I just want to
call out that there

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:58.550
was a session two days
ago that goes much more

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:00.590
deeply into detail, and
all of these sessions we

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:01.790
posted on YouTube.

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:04.780
So check this out if
it's interesting to you.

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:07.870
Another tool that we
offer here at Google Cloud

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:10.180
is the idea of
custom machine types.

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:12.010
The general purpose
line of VMs that I just

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:14.530
talked about, they do come
with some cookie-cutter

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:15.603
configurations.

00:05:15.603 --> 00:05:17.020
But at the same
time, you actually

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:20.260
have the flexibility to change
the shape of the machine

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:23.530
in increments of two
vCPUs, or increments

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:24.910
of one gigabyte in memory.

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:27.198
And we find that,
especially as customers

00:05:27.198 --> 00:05:28.990
start to round the
corner of Cloud adoption

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:30.823
and become more mature
in their deployments,

00:05:30.823 --> 00:05:32.770
that they can get a lot
of bang for their buck

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:36.038
by right-sizing their machines
using custom machine types.

00:05:36.038 --> 00:05:38.080
So for example, if you
start with a cookie-cutter

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:39.880
configuration but
then you realize

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:41.800
that you're underutilizing
your memory,

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:44.787
you can just scale back the
memory for that instance.

00:05:44.787 --> 00:05:46.870
You shut down the instance,
scale back the memory,

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:48.790
and then turn it back
on again, and you'll

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:51.463
see that the memory
has gone down.

00:05:51.463 --> 00:05:52.630
It's really easy to do this.

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:55.172
If you go into the UI, you'll
just see a set of sliders here,

00:05:55.172 --> 00:05:57.820
or G Cloud, you can use the
custom CPU, a custom memory

00:05:57.820 --> 00:05:59.903
flag.

00:05:59.903 --> 00:06:01.820
Another use case we hear
from customers a lot,

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:04.550
is they might have
fault-tolerant jobs,

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:07.370
or short-lived tasks
that they need to do.

00:06:07.370 --> 00:06:10.960
Maybe they don't really care
when these tasks happen,

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.470
and maybe these tasks are kind
of embarrassingly parallel.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:16.520
It's a little bit of computation
that you can spin up, finish,

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:18.680
and then go on.

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:22.550
Examples of this include
rendering or genomics, or media

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:24.410
trance coding, and
for those use cases,

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:27.500
we have the preemptible
machine VMs.

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:29.540
So preemtible VMs,
they're offered

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:31.970
in the same machine types and
machines shapes that I just

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:33.230
talked about.

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:35.870
They'll last for up
to 24 hours, but they

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:40.010
can be preempted within that
24-hour time period with a 30

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:41.900
second notification.

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:43.850
The tradeoff being
that they're up to 80%

00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:45.923
cheaper than regular instances.

00:06:45.923 --> 00:06:47.340
And so we hear a
lot of customers,

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:48.840
when they start
their Cloud journey,

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:52.260
they'll make everything a
regular or non-preemtible VM.

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:55.260
But as they deploy, they start
to identify certain workloads

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:57.000
that fit this
criteria, and then they

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.570
can migrate those jobs to
be pre-emptive instead.

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:04.513
And in doing so,
save a lot of money.

00:07:04.513 --> 00:07:06.180
You can think of it
as a setting on a VM

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:07.710
when you create it
and you can just

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:10.890
set the preemptible flag there,
and you're off to the races

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:14.670
with a preemptible VM.

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:17.350
As we've kind of matured
with our customers,

00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:19.320
another thing we
started to hear is

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:22.560
that some customers are
really sensitive to what

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:24.250
they call single-host tendency.

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:27.630
So they say, I'm happy
moving to the Cloud,

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:29.335
but the host machines
that host my VMs,

00:07:29.335 --> 00:07:33.310
I don't want them hosting
VMs from other customers.

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:36.600
Now, this might be because they
have regulatory or compliance

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:40.020
requirements that evolved
in an on-premises world,

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:42.690
which meant that that's
how they've always--

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:44.190
this is how they've
always operated,

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:46.398
and they don't want to go
and change those compliance

00:07:46.398 --> 00:07:48.210
requirements, or
this might be because

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.340
of licensing limitations.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:52.980
For those use cases,
we've recently GA'd what

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:55.330
we call sole-tenant nodes.

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:58.420
So this is the ability to
take a physical server,

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:02.187
and reserve it just for your
use cases and just for your VMs.

00:08:02.187 --> 00:08:03.270
So what does it look like?

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:06.048
On the left here, we
see a normal host.

00:08:06.048 --> 00:08:07.340
This is a non-sole-tenant node.

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:09.900
This is what you've
always been using on GCE.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:12.750
We manage the host
hardware in both cases,

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:15.390
but in this particular
case, other customers

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:17.550
may have VMs that are also
running in the same host

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:18.270
hardware.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:20.190
We, of course, manage
the isolation for you,

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:21.760
and all that.

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:24.450
But if you have certain
requirements that I just

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:27.372
talked about, you can move
on to a sole-tenant host.

00:08:27.372 --> 00:08:28.830
There, what's
happening is, you now

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:32.400
have this machine reserved
exclusively for your VMs.

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:34.830
But you should
note, that you still

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:36.659
have the flexibility
within that machine

00:08:36.659 --> 00:08:38.669
to set up VMs of
different shapes and sizes

00:08:38.669 --> 00:08:41.565
and to change the configurations
of sizes as needed.

00:08:41.565 --> 00:08:42.940
This is a bit of
a newer feature.

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:45.210
We're just starting to
see some adoption there.

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:46.920
But I want to walk through
what that looks like so you

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:49.003
can get a sense of how
easy this is to actually go

00:08:49.003 --> 00:08:50.110
ahead and deploy it.

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:52.937
So we're inside the
developer console of the UI.

00:08:52.937 --> 00:08:55.020
All of these features that
I'm going to talk about

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:58.273
are accessible from the UI,
and the G Cloud, and the API

00:08:58.273 --> 00:09:00.690
surface, but I think the UI
just makes it a little clearer

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:02.640
sometimes to walk through.

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:04.380
Here we are in the UI.

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:06.060
We're in the Compute
Engine section,

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:07.770
we click on sole-tenant nodes.

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:10.520
You can see I'm asked
to just create one.

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:13.598
In creating one, I'm just
asked for a few configuration

00:09:13.598 --> 00:09:15.390
settings, like what do
you want to call it,

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:17.260
and how big do you
want the machine to be.

00:09:17.260 --> 00:09:19.270
It's just a few-step process.

00:09:19.270 --> 00:09:21.690
And once I've created it,
you'll see an entry here.

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:24.000
Kind of hard to see, but
basically, that line entry

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.520
there represents a
single sole-tenant host,

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.120
ready to host your VMs.

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:30.120
Here you can see this
particular configuration--

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:31.860
or maybe you can't
see it, but it

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:35.520
has 96 vCPUs, and over
600 gigabytes of RAM

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:37.380
and you can fit as
many VMs in there

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:41.010
as you want that fit
within that configuration.

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:43.500
From there, when
you create a VM,

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:47.550
you can just specify the
sole-tenant node group

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:49.500
that you want the
VM to fit into,

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:51.690
and everything else is the same.

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:54.960
Every other part of managing
that VM is the same as before,

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:57.090
same as you're used to,
as long as you specify

00:09:57.090 --> 00:09:59.610
that particular node group,
which you can see in the G

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:01.760
Cloud command over there.

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:03.510
You'll find that the
VM is now going to be

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:04.890
hosted in dedicated hardware.

00:10:07.318 --> 00:10:09.360
One thing that we think
this will really help on,

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:14.340
is it'll allow you to bring
your existing licenses to GCP.

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:17.620
As we've been working with
enterprise-grade customers,

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:20.700
we found that many of them have,
for example, Microsoft software

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:22.830
licenses, already-purchased;
Microsoft Windows

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:24.450
licenses, already purchased.

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:28.920
And that the cost for
them on GCE, up until now,

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:30.480
sometimes the cost
for the licensing

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:34.560
was actually overshadowing
the costs of the vCPU and RAM

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:35.942
for these customers.

00:10:35.942 --> 00:10:37.650
One of our biggest
goals in the past year

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:40.590
was to allow you to take these
licenses that you had already

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:43.110
purchased from Microsoft or
from other software vendors,

00:10:43.110 --> 00:10:46.650
and apply them to GCP so that
you don't have to rebuy them

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:49.440
when you're on GCP.

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:51.210
At Next, we
announced the ability

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:52.650
to bring your own license.

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:54.300
This is in BETA.

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:59.670
And the way this works is, you
can set up a sole-tenant node,

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:02.610
and in doing so, you'll be
compliant with the Microsoft

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:04.170
bring your own
license requirements

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:07.140
for dedicated hardware
and per core licensing.

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:09.810
And in doing so,
you can then apply

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:11.660
the licenses you've
already purchased

00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:14.790
to your GCE deployment.

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:16.040
So you have some options here.

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:18.490
I want to walk
through some of them.

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:20.140
So this option actually
already existed

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:22.992
even before we did this work,
but in talking with customers,

00:11:22.992 --> 00:11:24.700
we found that very
few customers actually

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:26.380
realized this was
an option, so I just

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:27.675
want to reiterate it here.

00:11:27.675 --> 00:11:29.800
So, in this option you
don't use sole-tenant nodes,

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:32.800
you just use our typical
multi-tenant hardware.

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:36.400
And it turns out, if you
have software assurance

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:39.130
with Microsoft, you can use
the license mobility program

00:11:39.130 --> 00:11:43.600
to bring software
licenses into GCP already.

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:45.172
For example, for SQL Server.

00:11:45.172 --> 00:11:46.630
In this configuration,
you're still

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:49.720
paying for the premium
Windows images in GCP,

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:52.350
but you can at least bring
your software licenses

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:56.200
to your GCP hardware.

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:58.545
Option two, this is the
one we're announcing here,

00:11:58.545 --> 00:12:00.670
is you would use the
sole-tenant nodes that we just

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:01.750
talked about.

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:04.240
Those sole-tenant nodes are
compliant with the Microsoft

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:06.430
bring your own license
requirements, such

00:12:06.430 --> 00:12:09.640
that they allow you to bring
both your Windows licenses

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:14.080
and your Microsoft
software licenses onto GCP,

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:18.100
and you don't need to
have software assurance

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:20.003
from Microsoft in
order to do so.

00:12:20.003 --> 00:12:21.670
We're already seeing
some customers move

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:23.420
on to this particular
model, and it really

00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:26.440
allows them to get the mileage
from their existing software

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:31.930
licenses, and apply that
to apply that to GCP.

00:12:31.930 --> 00:12:34.100
OK, so the next section
I want to talk about

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:35.927
is around managing your data.

00:12:35.927 --> 00:12:38.010
So before, we were talking
about virtual machines.

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:40.052
Now I want to talk about
the disks and the images

00:12:40.052 --> 00:12:42.500
and the snapshots that you
have in Compute Engine,

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:44.540
and the tools that we
offer you to manage them

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:47.085
and to give you some
guardrails behind them.

00:12:47.085 --> 00:12:48.710
The first thing--
and this is something

00:12:48.710 --> 00:12:51.120
we've been hearing
increasingly from customers,

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:53.090
especially those in
regulated industries,

00:12:53.090 --> 00:12:54.920
is they might have
some compliance

00:12:54.920 --> 00:12:57.110
or regulatory
requirements around,

00:12:57.110 --> 00:12:59.900
I need to control how
my data is encrypted

00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:02.570
and how my data is
managed in Compute Engine.

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:05.990
I need to make sure that,
for example, if I had to,

00:13:05.990 --> 00:13:08.630
I can totally revoke
access for that data

00:13:08.630 --> 00:13:11.150
from everybody, including
the Cloud provider, including

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:12.770
Google.

00:13:12.770 --> 00:13:15.290
A practice that's increasingly
being adopted we're seeing

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:18.200
is the use of--

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:21.350
so what happens is the customers
can create keys and Cloud

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:22.550
key management service.

00:13:22.550 --> 00:13:25.340
That's a managed service that
we offer for key management

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:26.540
and key storage.

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:29.180
And what you can do is
you go ahead and create

00:13:29.180 --> 00:13:32.870
a key in Cloud KMS,
you point GCE to it,

00:13:32.870 --> 00:13:35.270
and then GCE will
automatically start

00:13:35.270 --> 00:13:40.540
encrypting your disks and images
and snapshots with those keys.

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:42.050
And at any moment,
you can actually

00:13:42.050 --> 00:13:46.540
revoke access of those keys from
GCE, and what that means is GCE

00:13:46.540 --> 00:13:49.850
will stop having
access to the data.

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:52.600
So let's walk through that a
little bit, just a bit more

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:53.500
concrete detail.

00:13:53.500 --> 00:13:56.350
You would first start by
creating a key ring and a key

00:13:56.350 --> 00:13:57.220
in Cloud KMS.

00:13:57.220 --> 00:13:58.870
That's a very quick process.

00:13:58.870 --> 00:14:00.820
You would specify, for
example, the rotation

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:04.360
period and the name of the
key and where it lives.

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:06.550
And then from there,
when in GCE, you

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:09.370
go to actually create a
disk or image or snapshot,

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:11.800
you'll find an option
to point GCE to the key

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:13.180
that you just created.

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:16.900
Here in the UI, you can see
it's one of the few options.

00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:18.040
You have G Cloud as well.

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:19.660
You have the KMS
key flag, where you

00:14:19.660 --> 00:14:23.020
can specify the URI for the
key, and that's and that's

00:14:23.020 --> 00:14:23.930
basically it.

00:14:23.930 --> 00:14:25.540
Once you've done
that, you've told

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:28.240
GCE to protect your disks
and images and snapshots

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:30.720
with that particular key.

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:34.000
And from there, you can
revoke access as needed.

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:35.750
You can do that in the
UI by just clicking

00:14:35.750 --> 00:14:37.375
on the disable option
there, or there's

00:14:37.375 --> 00:14:40.440
a disabled command and G Cloud.

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:43.200
And from there, you'll find a
Compute Engine now no longer

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:45.560
has access to that data.

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:48.060
For example, if you took that
disk and you tried mounting it

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:53.190
to a VM, or if the disk is was
already mounted onto the VM

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:54.840
and you started
it, you would find

00:14:54.840 --> 00:15:01.540
that those actions would fail
until the key was restored.

00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:04.030
Another thing we've
heard from customers,

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:06.630
especially those
in other regions,

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:09.420
is they want the ability
to store all data

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:11.110
in a particular location.

00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:13.365
So for example, we have a
lot of customers in Europe,

00:15:13.365 --> 00:15:15.240
and what they tell us
is, I need to make sure

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:17.700
that none of my data
lives in anywhere else

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:20.550
other than the European Union.

00:15:20.550 --> 00:15:23.760
Up until this point,
persistent disks

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:26.940
have always been regional
or zonal resources.

00:15:26.940 --> 00:15:29.340
That meant you have
control over exactly

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:31.080
where those disks lived.

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:33.822
Up until this point, though,
images and snapshots--

00:15:33.822 --> 00:15:35.280
so images are used
to create disks,

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:37.440
and snapshots are used
to backup disk state--

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:39.330
they have been global resources.

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:41.880
And so that's been convenient
for say, a global software

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:43.980
deployment, where you can
have a global deployment

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:45.960
and a single image
resource that's global

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:48.390
and it's supporting
that entire deployment,

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:51.210
or backups that support
a global deployment.

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:53.370
But customers have
been asking us

00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:56.070
for a lot more flexibility
in being able to say,

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:58.110
I want this particular
image or snapshot

00:15:58.110 --> 00:16:01.140
to live in a particular
region or location,

00:16:01.140 --> 00:16:03.480
and only live in that location.

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:05.940
We're announcing
here the ability

00:16:05.940 --> 00:16:09.750
to specify storage locations
for both images and snapshots.

00:16:09.750 --> 00:16:12.030
Now, for snapshots, it
turns out that's already

00:16:12.030 --> 00:16:13.560
been released to BETA.

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:15.640
We did that in the
lead up to Next.

00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:18.610
But we're also going to for
sure be doing that for images

00:16:18.610 --> 00:16:19.110
as well.

00:16:19.110 --> 00:16:22.725
That's coming soon to alpha.

00:16:22.725 --> 00:16:24.350
The way this works
is it's very simple.

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:26.190
There should be a
minimal influence

00:16:26.190 --> 00:16:27.990
to any of your workflows.

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:29.200
But if you go into--

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:30.970
so, we're back in the
Compute Engine UI.

00:16:30.970 --> 00:16:32.220
This is the list of snapshots.

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:34.945
We'll do this for the
list of images as well.

00:16:34.945 --> 00:16:36.570
It will just tell
you for each snapshot

00:16:36.570 --> 00:16:40.770
now, where that actual storage
location of the data is.

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:43.237
And similarly, when you're
creating that image,

00:16:43.237 --> 00:16:44.820
or when you're
creating that snapshot,

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:46.660
you'll have an
option now to say,

00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:49.320
this is where I want that
image or snapshot to live.

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:50.798
If you don't
specify that option,

00:16:50.798 --> 00:16:52.590
it'll continue to be
multi-regional the way

00:16:52.590 --> 00:16:53.760
it always was.

00:16:53.760 --> 00:16:56.660
You can see the associated
G Cloud command there.

00:16:56.660 --> 00:17:01.420
It's fairly easy, but
much more flexibility.

00:17:01.420 --> 00:17:03.300
Another thing we
saw customers do

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:06.329
is they're using snapshots
to make sure they have

00:17:06.329 --> 00:17:08.760
backups of the state of VMs.

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:11.339
And what we saw a
lot, was a pattern

00:17:11.339 --> 00:17:13.920
of customers writing
their own automation,

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:15.660
writing their own
tooling to make sure

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:19.460
that these snapshots were
generated on a regular basis.

00:17:19.460 --> 00:17:22.750
Say every day, or every
week, or every month.

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:25.829
And we saw so much of this and
so much of these custom tools,

00:17:25.829 --> 00:17:28.590
that we thought, hey, you
know what, we should actually

00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:31.320
offer an easy way for you to
do this that's Google managed

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:34.830
and that reduces the maintenance
overhead on your side.

00:17:34.830 --> 00:17:36.600
Leading up to
Next, we introduced

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:38.640
what we call scheduled
snapshots to BETA.

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:40.920
So this is a publicly
available feature now.

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:42.863
We highly encourage
you to go play with it.

00:17:42.863 --> 00:17:44.280
And what this will
do is it allows

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:48.420
you to tell us, the platform,
to automatically take snapshots

00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:50.872
for you on a regular basis.

00:17:50.872 --> 00:17:52.330
Here's what that
looks like, so now

00:17:52.330 --> 00:17:54.410
if you go into the
snapshots view,

00:17:54.410 --> 00:17:56.990
you'll see a new tab here
called snapshots schedules.

00:17:56.990 --> 00:18:00.400
This is where you can
create and modify schedules.

00:18:00.400 --> 00:18:02.500
If I were to go and
create a schedule,

00:18:02.500 --> 00:18:04.360
I could specify a
bunch of things.

00:18:04.360 --> 00:18:08.050
For example, when do I want
this schedule to execute,

00:18:08.050 --> 00:18:11.200
how long do I want these
snapshots to stick around for,

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:14.150
what should I do if the disk is
deleted, things of that sort.

00:18:14.150 --> 00:18:17.080
So here, in particular, I'm
creating a snapshot schedule

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:22.180
that's set to run daily and to
take snapshots at around noon.

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:25.570
Once I have that schedule, I
can then click into a disk,

00:18:25.570 --> 00:18:27.400
so here I am in
the lists of disks.

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:31.330
I can click into that, and then
apply the snapshot schedule

00:18:31.330 --> 00:18:31.900
to the disk.

00:18:31.900 --> 00:18:35.230
So here I am inside the
edit disk page here,

00:18:35.230 --> 00:18:38.290
and I apply the snapshot
schedule that I just created.

00:18:38.290 --> 00:18:39.050
Easy as that.

00:18:39.050 --> 00:18:41.050
And from here, you'll
see that Compute Engine

00:18:41.050 --> 00:18:45.760
is going to automatically start
applying that schedule for you.

00:18:45.760 --> 00:18:48.140
These snapshot schedules,
they don't charge.

00:18:48.140 --> 00:18:50.623
There's no charge associated
to creating these schedules.

00:18:50.623 --> 00:18:52.540
You're just going to be
paying for the storage

00:18:52.540 --> 00:18:54.412
of the snapshots
as you always have.

00:18:54.412 --> 00:18:55.870
And the other nice
thing about this

00:18:55.870 --> 00:18:58.422
is snapshots are
differential in nature, which

00:18:58.422 --> 00:19:00.130
means when you take
a snapshot, it's only

00:19:00.130 --> 00:19:02.980
storing the differential
between the current time

00:19:02.980 --> 00:19:04.217
and the previous snapshot.

00:19:04.217 --> 00:19:05.800
What that means is
if your disk is not

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:07.780
changing very much in
between the snapshots,

00:19:07.780 --> 00:19:09.993
you won't be charged very
much and in particular,

00:19:09.993 --> 00:19:11.410
if the disk doesn't
change at all,

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:14.115
you won't be charged at all.

00:19:14.115 --> 00:19:16.240
So another powerful tool--
we just rolled this out.

00:19:16.240 --> 00:19:18.590
We're already seeing
some great adoption

00:19:18.590 --> 00:19:20.720
from some of our early
adopters, and they've

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:23.450
said that it's been able to
allow them to basically throw

00:19:23.450 --> 00:19:25.220
away all of the custom
tooling and scripts

00:19:25.220 --> 00:19:28.040
that they've built around this.

00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:33.890
OK, so that was a recap of
some of the new features

00:19:33.890 --> 00:19:38.270
and a few of the best
practices around GCE,

00:19:38.270 --> 00:19:39.890
but a common question
we get asked,

00:19:39.890 --> 00:19:41.432
kind of around this
point as we start

00:19:41.432 --> 00:19:44.450
to talk about best practices
is, how do I make sure

00:19:44.450 --> 00:19:46.040
that the people in
my organization,

00:19:46.040 --> 00:19:48.170
that developers my
organization, are actually

00:19:48.170 --> 00:19:50.100
following these best practices?

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:52.740
How do I monitor what's
going on in my organization?

00:19:52.740 --> 00:19:54.200
And then, how do
I start to enforce

00:19:54.200 --> 00:19:56.810
some certain set of best
practices or policies,

00:19:56.810 --> 00:19:58.550
and start to put
up those guardrails

00:19:58.550 --> 00:20:00.350
so that I can make
sure that people

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:02.270
are doing the right thing?

00:20:02.270 --> 00:20:05.400
And to motivate this, I want to
talk about sprawl for a second.

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:07.430
So this here is a
picture of urban sprawl.

00:20:07.430 --> 00:20:10.130
This is San Francisco
looking out over the horizon.

00:20:10.130 --> 00:20:12.470
And you can see there's
buildings, buildings,

00:20:12.470 --> 00:20:13.843
and buildings everywhere.

00:20:13.843 --> 00:20:15.260
Similarly, when
we've been working

00:20:15.260 --> 00:20:17.690
with some of our enterprise
customers, what we see

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:19.830
is the concept of Cloud sprawl.

00:20:19.830 --> 00:20:22.670
So you open up GCP
to your developers,

00:20:22.670 --> 00:20:25.160
and almost overnight,
what we've seen

00:20:25.160 --> 00:20:27.440
is you have hundreds or
thousands of projects spring

00:20:27.440 --> 00:20:30.090
up, because every developer
gets their hands on it.

00:20:30.090 --> 00:20:31.820
They do some code
labs or tutorials,

00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:33.950
every time they do that,
a new project spins up.

00:20:33.950 --> 00:20:36.020
All of a sudden you have
thousands of projects.

00:20:36.020 --> 00:20:37.700
And in that
environment, it can be

00:20:37.700 --> 00:20:41.240
very difficult to wrap
your head around what there

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:43.370
is in your
organization, and also

00:20:43.370 --> 00:20:45.800
to make sure that people are
following the best practices.

00:20:45.800 --> 00:20:49.295
It can just devolve
into a bit of a mess.

00:20:49.295 --> 00:20:51.170
And so that's what this
section is all about,

00:20:51.170 --> 00:20:53.240
is how do you make
sense of that mess.

00:20:53.240 --> 00:20:54.833
How do you start to
govern that mess.

00:20:54.833 --> 00:20:56.750
Part of the mess is good,
because it's agility

00:20:56.750 --> 00:20:58.250
and it's people moving
quickly, but you

00:20:58.250 --> 00:21:00.792
want to make sure that, again
they're not falling off a cliff

00:21:00.792 --> 00:21:01.578
and doing so.

00:21:01.578 --> 00:21:03.620
The first thing I want to
talk about a little bit

00:21:03.620 --> 00:21:05.810
is the resource hierarchy.

00:21:05.810 --> 00:21:07.310
What we found working
with customers

00:21:07.310 --> 00:21:10.640
is that building a
proper resource hierarchy

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:13.130
is a really, really great
force multiplier for you

00:21:13.130 --> 00:21:15.980
to help you understand
what all is going on

00:21:15.980 --> 00:21:18.150
in your organization.

00:21:18.150 --> 00:21:19.650
This is a 300 level
talk, so I'm not

00:21:19.650 --> 00:21:21.775
going to dwell too much on
this particular diagram,

00:21:21.775 --> 00:21:23.370
although I hope
you've seen it before.

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:26.850
But in general, I just want to
reiterate that in Google Cloud,

00:21:26.850 --> 00:21:30.590
you have resources like the
VMs and disks in subnets.

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:32.460
Those roll up into projects.

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:35.340
So every resource
must have a project.

00:21:35.340 --> 00:21:37.980
And then projects roll up
optionally into folders,

00:21:37.980 --> 00:21:40.703
and then folders roll
up into organizations.

00:21:40.703 --> 00:21:42.120
And what happens
here is, when you

00:21:42.120 --> 00:21:45.920
set a policy on a
particular say, folder,

00:21:45.920 --> 00:21:48.570
it'll roll down into the
projects, and then from there,

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:50.118
it'll roll down into resources.

00:21:50.118 --> 00:21:51.660
And what that lets
you do is, it lets

00:21:51.660 --> 00:21:54.090
you start grouping
resources that you

00:21:54.090 --> 00:21:57.090
want to have similar policies
on together so that you

00:21:57.090 --> 00:22:00.760
don't have to reason over
those resources individually.

00:22:00.760 --> 00:22:03.280
And once you have that
resource hierarchy,

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:06.370
it allows you to just have a
lot more human comprehensibility

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:07.960
around your decisions.

00:22:07.960 --> 00:22:10.240
So that was super abstract.

00:22:10.240 --> 00:22:12.010
Recently I was
talking to a customer,

00:22:12.010 --> 00:22:13.900
and this is one of
our biggest customers,

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:16.750
and the person
said, well, we heard

00:22:16.750 --> 00:22:19.820
that spiel like, 100
times, even still,

00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:22.070
after two years of
deploying Google Cloud,

00:22:22.070 --> 00:22:24.070
we still don't really
have a resource hierarchy.

00:22:24.070 --> 00:22:26.195
We just have thousands of
projects all reporting up

00:22:26.195 --> 00:22:28.010
into a single organization.

00:22:28.010 --> 00:22:30.290
And when I asked the
individual why, they said,

00:22:30.290 --> 00:22:32.960
well, we know what a folder
is, we knew what a project is,

00:22:32.960 --> 00:22:34.960
we never really just had
an understanding of how

00:22:34.960 --> 00:22:36.940
to put it all together,
or any case studies

00:22:36.940 --> 00:22:37.857
or anything like that.

00:22:37.857 --> 00:22:40.398
So I'm glad to report that that
particular individual has now

00:22:40.398 --> 00:22:40.900
actually--

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:43.318
that organization has gone
on and built a very effective

00:22:43.318 --> 00:22:44.110
resource hierarchy.

00:22:44.110 --> 00:22:46.540
But in case any of you
are in that same boat,

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:49.100
I wanted to share a much
more concrete example.

00:22:49.100 --> 00:22:52.480
And this is how
Google internally

00:22:52.480 --> 00:22:54.880
has structured our
resource hierarchy.

00:22:54.880 --> 00:22:59.860
So it turns out we have over
4 and 1/2 million projects

00:22:59.860 --> 00:23:01.990
reporting up into
Google.com, and you

00:23:01.990 --> 00:23:05.140
can imagine in that
situation how much of a mess

00:23:05.140 --> 00:23:07.630
it might be if you had to reason
over all of those projects

00:23:07.630 --> 00:23:08.638
individually.

00:23:08.638 --> 00:23:10.930
And so we've done some things
with our folder hierarchy

00:23:10.930 --> 00:23:12.490
to try to make sense of that.

00:23:12.490 --> 00:23:14.350
So the first thing we
have is the concept

00:23:14.350 --> 00:23:16.870
of what we call the
Google default folder.

00:23:16.870 --> 00:23:18.460
This is a folder
that encapsulates

00:23:18.460 --> 00:23:22.270
all of the sandbox projects that
our developers end up creating.

00:23:22.270 --> 00:23:25.570
And what that means
is we can start

00:23:25.570 --> 00:23:27.400
reasoning over those
sandbox projects

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:29.830
together, rather
than one by one.

00:23:29.830 --> 00:23:34.630
So we limit our
end users to only

00:23:34.630 --> 00:23:37.060
be able to create
projects willy-nilly

00:23:37.060 --> 00:23:38.590
in this particular folder.

00:23:38.590 --> 00:23:41.710
That means we know this folder
has all the sandbox projects,

00:23:41.710 --> 00:23:44.860
and in turn, we can then apply
the policies that make sense

00:23:44.860 --> 00:23:46.330
on these sandbox projects.

00:23:46.330 --> 00:23:48.328
For example, we can
apply a policy that says,

00:23:48.328 --> 00:23:50.620
well you know what, sandbox
projects probably shouldn't

00:23:50.620 --> 00:23:51.910
have external IP addresses.

00:23:51.910 --> 00:23:53.452
They probably
shouldn't be accessible

00:23:53.452 --> 00:23:54.760
from the public internet.

00:23:54.760 --> 00:23:56.295
Or something like,
sandbox projects,

00:23:56.295 --> 00:23:58.420
they probably shouldn't
have VMs that are too large

00:23:58.420 --> 00:24:02.140
or that are running overnight,
or off business hours,

00:24:02.140 --> 00:24:05.680
because maybe there's not
a much use case for that.

00:24:05.680 --> 00:24:07.240
So continuing along
those lines, we

00:24:07.240 --> 00:24:10.520
have another set
of like policies,

00:24:10.520 --> 00:24:12.560
which is for demo projects.

00:24:12.560 --> 00:24:15.498
So in this particular case,
we have a bunch of demos,

00:24:15.498 --> 00:24:17.290
maybe they're being
built by the sales team

00:24:17.290 --> 00:24:18.850
with the developer
relations team.

00:24:18.850 --> 00:24:20.680
We grouped them into a folder.

00:24:20.680 --> 00:24:23.333
Some policies will
apply for all demos.

00:24:23.333 --> 00:24:25.000
For example, it's
probably OK for a demo

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:27.800
to have an external IP
address in some cases.

00:24:27.800 --> 00:24:29.440
It's probably OK for
you to share demos

00:24:29.440 --> 00:24:32.480
with external audiences
in some cases.

00:24:32.480 --> 00:24:35.755
What we found is that there are
also some differences between

00:24:35.755 --> 00:24:37.630
how different teams want
to handle the demos,

00:24:37.630 --> 00:24:41.652
and so we split into
some sub-folders below--

00:24:41.652 --> 00:24:44.110
one for the sales team, and
one for the developer relations

00:24:44.110 --> 00:24:44.620
team--

00:24:44.620 --> 00:24:47.050
so that we can delegate
some additional access

00:24:47.050 --> 00:24:50.620
to those teams, and say, you
get to manage your policies

00:24:50.620 --> 00:24:52.980
within this folder.

00:24:52.980 --> 00:24:56.640
Another thing we noticed is that
the dev and test environments

00:24:56.640 --> 00:24:58.200
for our different
engineering teams

00:24:58.200 --> 00:25:00.340
all tend to sort
of look the same.

00:25:00.340 --> 00:25:01.980
And so we have a teams folder.

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:03.780
There we apply some
general policies

00:25:03.780 --> 00:25:05.880
for what dev and tests
should look like,

00:25:05.880 --> 00:25:08.010
but then we also have
individual team folders

00:25:08.010 --> 00:25:10.050
that our individual
teams can manage,

00:25:10.050 --> 00:25:13.228
and then we delegate access to.

00:25:13.228 --> 00:25:14.770
Another thing that
we found at Google

00:25:14.770 --> 00:25:17.080
is that we do have
some subsidiaries,

00:25:17.080 --> 00:25:18.730
some acquisitions.

00:25:18.730 --> 00:25:21.950
We do want to group
them under the top level

00:25:21.950 --> 00:25:24.645
org, because we want
to keep tabs on them,

00:25:24.645 --> 00:25:26.770
and because we want to
still be able to reason over

00:25:26.770 --> 00:25:29.500
all of the resources
in our organization.

00:25:29.500 --> 00:25:31.990
But then we break them off
into sub-folders like Verily

00:25:31.990 --> 00:25:34.270
in Google X, and then
give broad delegation

00:25:34.270 --> 00:25:37.695
administrative powers to
those particular subsidiaries.

00:25:37.695 --> 00:25:39.070
And this is a
pattern that we see

00:25:39.070 --> 00:25:41.110
happen a lot for other
companies as well

00:25:41.110 --> 00:25:44.060
when they deal
with acquisitions.

00:25:44.060 --> 00:25:47.300
And then finally, we have
the production folder.

00:25:47.300 --> 00:25:50.360
The production folder we found
just like with dev and test,

00:25:50.360 --> 00:25:53.720
production tends to have a
similar set of policies applied

00:25:53.720 --> 00:25:55.020
to them.

00:25:55.020 --> 00:25:58.340
So we apply those policies
to the top level production

00:25:58.340 --> 00:26:01.640
folder, where they go down into
all of the folders, sub-folders

00:26:01.640 --> 00:26:02.870
and sub-resources.

00:26:02.870 --> 00:26:06.110
But then we do have individual
services as well in there.

00:26:06.110 --> 00:26:11.330
And then we allow the individual
teams access to those folders

00:26:11.330 --> 00:26:14.618
and we allowed them to set their
own policies there as well.

00:26:14.618 --> 00:26:16.910
So this is a simplified
version of a resource hierarchy

00:26:16.910 --> 00:26:18.200
that we use at Google.

00:26:18.200 --> 00:26:22.040
It's also by far not the
only way to do it, for sure,

00:26:22.040 --> 00:26:24.350
but I hope this gives
you an example of how

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:26.805
you can take 4 and
1/2 million projects

00:26:26.805 --> 00:26:29.180
and start grouping them into
folders in a way that allows

00:26:29.180 --> 00:26:31.040
you to reason over
them more easily,

00:26:31.040 --> 00:26:35.170
and allow you to set policies
over them more easily.

00:26:35.170 --> 00:26:38.650
OK, so once you have
a resource hierarchy,

00:26:38.650 --> 00:26:41.160
another question that
comes is, how do I

00:26:41.160 --> 00:26:45.240
more easily see all that's
going on in my organization?

00:26:45.240 --> 00:26:47.220
This is a problem
regardless of how

00:26:47.220 --> 00:26:49.170
well-structured your
research hierarchy is,

00:26:49.170 --> 00:26:50.857
although the
hierarchy does help.

00:26:50.857 --> 00:26:52.440
I want to talk about
the tools that we

00:26:52.440 --> 00:26:55.350
offer you to help
monitor your enterprise,

00:26:55.350 --> 00:26:57.510
to help see what's going
on inside that sprawl,

00:26:57.510 --> 00:27:02.790
and who's creating virtual
machines, who is creating data,

00:27:02.790 --> 00:27:03.970
and things of that sort.

00:27:03.970 --> 00:27:05.460
So there's two tools
I want to talk about,

00:27:05.460 --> 00:27:07.627
and we're going to build
them up into some use cases

00:27:07.627 --> 00:27:08.680
as we go along.

00:27:08.680 --> 00:27:11.610
The first one is audit logs.

00:27:11.610 --> 00:27:14.060
So, audit logs are going
to give you a real time

00:27:14.060 --> 00:27:17.120
view of the individual
events that are going on

00:27:17.120 --> 00:27:18.480
in your organization.

00:27:18.480 --> 00:27:21.022
There are three types, and you
can think of these three types

00:27:21.022 --> 00:27:23.240
as combining together
to give you that view.

00:27:23.240 --> 00:27:25.578
The first type is
admin activity.

00:27:25.578 --> 00:27:27.620
These are going to log
the administrative actions

00:27:27.620 --> 00:27:29.870
that users are taking,
like creation and deletion

00:27:29.870 --> 00:27:31.980
and modification of resources.

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:34.230
The second type is
the system event.

00:27:34.230 --> 00:27:36.050
These are going
to log the events

00:27:36.050 --> 00:27:39.770
that GCE is taking on your
behalf to maintain your VMs.

00:27:39.770 --> 00:27:43.910
So for example, instance 01
was live-migrated at 1 PM.

00:27:43.910 --> 00:27:45.440
And the third type
is data access.

00:27:45.440 --> 00:27:48.930
This tracks not modifications,
but just reads of resources.

00:27:48.930 --> 00:27:51.650
So for example, Alice at
1 PM listed all the images

00:27:51.650 --> 00:27:56.830
in her project All of these
lines are created in real time

00:27:56.830 --> 00:27:58.048
with sub-second latencies.

00:27:58.048 --> 00:28:00.340
And that opens up some really
interesting possibilities

00:28:00.340 --> 00:28:02.632
as you start to ingest these
logs that we'll talk about

00:28:02.632 --> 00:28:03.490
in a second.

00:28:03.490 --> 00:28:04.960
There are also
immutable, so users

00:28:04.960 --> 00:28:07.750
can't go in and
actually change the logs

00:28:07.750 --> 00:28:10.440
after they were created.

00:28:10.440 --> 00:28:13.320
Admin activity logs-- the system
of logs will last for over

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:13.920
a year--

00:28:13.920 --> 00:28:16.710
by default, retained over
a year, and data access

00:28:16.710 --> 00:28:18.830
logs for 30 days.

00:28:18.830 --> 00:28:21.600
And all of these logs can
be exported to Cloud storage

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:23.687
BigQuery or Pub/Sub.

00:28:23.687 --> 00:28:25.520
Once you do that,
especially when you export

00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:27.778
to Cloud storage
or BigQuery-- now,

00:28:27.778 --> 00:28:30.320
obviously the retention policies
don't apply to them anymore.

00:28:30.320 --> 00:28:32.300
You can retain them
as long as you want.

00:28:32.300 --> 00:28:35.690
And for a Pub/Sub, this opens
up the door to some actions

00:28:35.690 --> 00:28:39.660
that you can take immediately
once autologs are generated.

00:28:39.660 --> 00:28:41.520
Admin activity logs
and system event logs

00:28:41.520 --> 00:28:44.520
are generated by
default. They're

00:28:44.520 --> 00:28:45.930
on by default for all projects.

00:28:45.930 --> 00:28:48.270
Whenever a user
creates a project,

00:28:48.270 --> 00:28:50.220
those audit logs
will start flowing.

00:28:50.220 --> 00:28:53.460
And finally, admin activity logs
and system event logs are not

00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:56.370
charged at all, whereas
data access logs,

00:28:56.370 --> 00:28:57.950
they are charged
by ingress volume.

00:28:57.950 --> 00:29:00.690
So data access logs, in
general, the best practice here

00:29:00.690 --> 00:29:03.633
is if you know you need it,
we suggest you turn them on.

00:29:03.633 --> 00:29:05.550
There's even a way to
blacklist certain users,

00:29:05.550 --> 00:29:08.020
so that they don't
generate data access logs.

00:29:08.020 --> 00:29:12.363
But you should be cautious of
the ingress volume charges.

00:29:12.363 --> 00:29:14.030
To make this a little
bit more concrete,

00:29:14.030 --> 00:29:16.877
we can take a look at what an
audit log actually looks like.

00:29:16.877 --> 00:29:18.710
We provide a number of
ways for you to go in

00:29:18.710 --> 00:29:21.242
and actually view
your audit logs.

00:29:21.242 --> 00:29:22.700
Here I am in the
developer console.

00:29:22.700 --> 00:29:24.950
I can go into the
logging section here,

00:29:24.950 --> 00:29:27.470
and I'll just see a list
of all the audit logs,

00:29:27.470 --> 00:29:31.157
and then I can filter
down that list as needed.

00:29:31.157 --> 00:29:32.990
And if you look into a
particular audit log,

00:29:32.990 --> 00:29:35.490
you'll find that we provide a
really rich set of information

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:36.230
for each.

00:29:36.230 --> 00:29:37.790
Here's a particular
admin activity

00:29:37.790 --> 00:29:40.730
log for a user that
stopped the GCE instance,

00:29:40.730 --> 00:29:44.420
and you'll see the email address
of the user, what they did,

00:29:44.420 --> 00:29:47.600
what resource they acted on,
and if they had any other,

00:29:47.600 --> 00:29:49.273
for example, fields
in the request body,

00:29:49.273 --> 00:29:50.690
you would see all
of that as well.

00:29:50.690 --> 00:29:52.440
And so, you really get
a lot of visibility

00:29:52.440 --> 00:29:56.290
into what users are doing
in your organization.

00:29:56.290 --> 00:29:59.030
And so a question that
tends to come up is,

00:29:59.030 --> 00:30:00.840
OK well, I have 4,000 projects.

00:30:00.840 --> 00:30:02.550
They're all
generating audit logs.

00:30:02.550 --> 00:30:04.020
Do I have to go
into each project

00:30:04.020 --> 00:30:05.730
to chase that
project down and make

00:30:05.730 --> 00:30:07.200
sure it's exporting somewhere?

00:30:07.200 --> 00:30:10.230
Or in other words, how can I
have all these other logs going

00:30:10.230 --> 00:30:12.990
to a single place, so that
no matter how many projects I

00:30:12.990 --> 00:30:15.510
have in my organization, no
matter how many folders I have,

00:30:15.510 --> 00:30:17.430
I can make sure
that I still have

00:30:17.430 --> 00:30:19.360
visibility over all of that.

00:30:19.360 --> 00:30:21.210
And so a best
practice here is, we

00:30:21.210 --> 00:30:23.550
have a feature called
aggregated logs export

00:30:23.550 --> 00:30:26.330
that lets you do that.

00:30:26.330 --> 00:30:27.960
With aggregated
logs export, you can

00:30:27.960 --> 00:30:30.570
specify at an organization
or a folder level

00:30:30.570 --> 00:30:33.480
that all of the projects beneath
that organization or folder

00:30:33.480 --> 00:30:37.380
are sending your audit logs
to a single centralized place.

00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:39.400
So here's the G Cloud
command to do that.

00:30:39.400 --> 00:30:41.970
So what we're saying is, G
Cloud logging syncs creates.

00:30:41.970 --> 00:30:44.070
So we're creating a new export.

00:30:44.070 --> 00:30:47.230
We're exporting to
a particular bucket,

00:30:47.230 --> 00:30:48.600
which is above this line.

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:50.250
This line says,
we want to export

00:30:50.250 --> 00:30:52.682
for this particular
organization.

00:30:52.682 --> 00:30:54.140
We're saying we
want to filter down

00:30:54.140 --> 00:30:55.723
to just the admin activity logs.

00:30:55.723 --> 00:30:57.140
Maybe that's what
we're interested

00:30:57.140 --> 00:30:58.540
in this particular example.

00:30:58.540 --> 00:31:01.040
And then we're saying, this is
the most important flag here,

00:31:01.040 --> 00:31:03.590
the include children
flag says, apply this

00:31:03.590 --> 00:31:06.200
to not only this
organization, but actually

00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:08.090
to all of the
projects and folders

00:31:08.090 --> 00:31:10.413
and resources underneath
that organization.

00:31:10.413 --> 00:31:12.080
With this one line,
we're able to answer

00:31:12.080 --> 00:31:13.455
that previous
question, and we're

00:31:13.455 --> 00:31:16.640
able to make sure that we have
a single GCS bucket with all

00:31:16.640 --> 00:31:19.410
of the events that are happening
in my entire organization

00:31:19.410 --> 00:31:21.350
and this is getting
updated in real time.

00:31:24.160 --> 00:31:25.810
A real world example
of what we've

00:31:25.810 --> 00:31:30.250
seen folks do with
audit logs is, well,

00:31:30.250 --> 00:31:32.020
here's a real world example.

00:31:32.020 --> 00:31:36.220
We have companies that are
really interested in reacting

00:31:36.220 --> 00:31:37.540
to insider risks.

00:31:37.540 --> 00:31:40.000
And sometimes they might
have an insider risks warning

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:44.110
around a user who may have had
their credentials compromised,

00:31:44.110 --> 00:31:48.220
who may be doing some unwanted
things inside the organization.

00:31:48.220 --> 00:31:50.350
With audit logs, and with
the search functionality

00:31:50.350 --> 00:31:53.200
in audit logs, you can use that
to easily filter down the audit

00:31:53.200 --> 00:31:56.720
logs to just the actions
that a particular user took.

00:31:56.720 --> 00:31:59.440
So in this particular
example, what we're doing is--

00:31:59.440 --> 00:32:01.130
for this G Cloud
command for example,

00:32:01.130 --> 00:32:03.580
we can say I want to
read the audit logs

00:32:03.580 --> 00:32:07.600
that just go against GCE
instances, that are just

00:32:07.600 --> 00:32:09.850
about admin activity,
and that just

00:32:09.850 --> 00:32:13.180
involve this particular Alice
user that I'm interested in.

00:32:13.180 --> 00:32:15.140
You can also zoom
in, for example,

00:32:15.140 --> 00:32:17.867
on a particular instance to see
the history of that instance.

00:32:17.867 --> 00:32:19.450
Now, in this particular
example, we're

00:32:19.450 --> 00:32:21.462
just looking at a
particular project.

00:32:21.462 --> 00:32:23.920
But you can imagine how you
can combine this particular use

00:32:23.920 --> 00:32:26.620
case with the previous one where
you're aggregating all the logs

00:32:26.620 --> 00:32:30.415
across your organization
to easily search

00:32:30.415 --> 00:32:32.290
against all the activities
that are happening

00:32:32.290 --> 00:32:34.070
in your organization.

00:32:34.070 --> 00:32:37.160
And we find that to be
a really powerful tool.

00:32:37.160 --> 00:32:40.310
Now, we released audit
logs a long time ago,

00:32:40.310 --> 00:32:42.710
and we've seen a
lot of adoption,

00:32:42.710 --> 00:32:44.510
but sometimes we
get the question of,

00:32:44.510 --> 00:32:46.580
I don't care so
much about the view

00:32:46.580 --> 00:32:48.500
of all the events
in the world, as I

00:32:48.500 --> 00:32:51.800
care about the state
of all of my resources

00:32:51.800 --> 00:32:53.460
at a given point in time.

00:32:53.460 --> 00:32:56.060
And this is sort of possible to
reconstruct using audit logs,

00:32:56.060 --> 00:32:57.680
but it takes a
little bit of work,

00:32:57.680 --> 00:33:01.440
and it's just not an easy
streamlined thing to do.

00:33:01.440 --> 00:33:04.010
And as a result,
we've introduced

00:33:04.010 --> 00:33:07.800
and we've GA'd very recently
the Cloud asset inventory API.

00:33:07.800 --> 00:33:10.610
So this is a powerful
API that will,

00:33:10.610 --> 00:33:13.820
for any given state in time
in the past five weeks,

00:33:13.820 --> 00:33:17.090
allow you to export what all
of your resources look like

00:33:17.090 --> 00:33:21.050
and what all the policies on
those resources look like.

00:33:21.050 --> 00:33:22.590
And so what does that look like?

00:33:22.590 --> 00:33:23.340
So these are APIs.

00:33:23.340 --> 00:33:25.828
So we'll be making
an API request

00:33:25.828 --> 00:33:26.870
appear in the first line.

00:33:26.870 --> 00:33:28.787
You can see we're
requesting the access token.

00:33:28.787 --> 00:33:31.210
That's just the canonical
way to get the access token,

00:33:31.210 --> 00:33:34.155
and then we attach it
onto the request here.

00:33:34.155 --> 00:33:35.530
And then what we
can do is, we're

00:33:35.530 --> 00:33:37.240
calling the Cloud
asset inventory

00:33:37.240 --> 00:33:39.740
APIs on a particular project.

00:33:39.740 --> 00:33:43.940
And we're saying export x
assets for that project.

00:33:43.940 --> 00:33:47.020
You can also call them on a
folder or on an organization,

00:33:47.020 --> 00:33:48.820
and it'll do the same thing.

00:33:48.820 --> 00:33:50.410
In this particular
case, we're asking

00:33:50.410 --> 00:33:54.740
for a resource as opposed to
resources and the policies.

00:33:54.740 --> 00:33:57.590
So we're saying, get all the
resources for this project,

00:33:57.590 --> 00:34:02.130
and then we're saying dump
that all into a GCS bucket.

00:34:02.130 --> 00:34:04.170
So once you've done
that, you'll see

00:34:04.170 --> 00:34:08.370
just a list of all of the
resources in that project.

00:34:08.370 --> 00:34:10.090
Now we didn't say
what time to do it,

00:34:10.090 --> 00:34:13.659
so the Cloud asset inventory is
assumed to do it for right now,

00:34:13.659 --> 00:34:16.708
but you can also specify at time
anytime in the past five weeks.

00:34:16.708 --> 00:34:18.250
This is what the
response looks like.

00:34:18.250 --> 00:34:21.389
So in blue, you'll see some
metadata about this entry.

00:34:21.389 --> 00:34:23.800
So this is basically us
saying, we found an instance.

00:34:23.800 --> 00:34:25.710
Its parented to
this project, this

00:34:25.710 --> 00:34:27.090
is the information about it.

00:34:27.090 --> 00:34:28.590
And then in green,
you can see we've

00:34:28.590 --> 00:34:31.360
printed out all the properties
of that particular resource.

00:34:31.360 --> 00:34:34.380
So you'll see what the CPU
platform is, the disks attached

00:34:34.380 --> 00:34:36.810
to it, basically every
property that you

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:41.739
would see from making a GET
request against that resource.

00:34:41.739 --> 00:34:45.989
So this API has just been GA'd.

00:34:45.989 --> 00:34:49.460
We've just started to work
with customers on this.

00:34:49.460 --> 00:34:51.060
But we've already
found that customers

00:34:51.060 --> 00:34:53.610
are using these
APIs to implement

00:34:53.610 --> 00:34:56.409
a pretty powerful and
wide range of use cases.

00:34:56.409 --> 00:34:59.560
So for example, you can, as
kind of the first thing to do,

00:34:59.560 --> 00:35:01.200
you can just see how
many projects have

00:35:01.200 --> 00:35:03.750
GCE resources inside of them.

00:35:03.750 --> 00:35:06.150
Working with one
customer, we asked

00:35:06.150 --> 00:35:08.670
them upfront before they did
this experiment, how many they

00:35:08.670 --> 00:35:10.980
thought there were and
they said around 100.

00:35:10.980 --> 00:35:13.800
It turns out they had
over 500 resources--

00:35:13.800 --> 00:35:17.370
500 projects, excuse
me, with GCE resources.

00:35:17.370 --> 00:35:19.470
They were really glad that
they made this request,

00:35:19.470 --> 00:35:21.470
and it's just another
example of the sprawl that

00:35:21.470 --> 00:35:25.363
happens inside organizations
that are rolling out the cloud.

00:35:25.363 --> 00:35:27.780
You can also do things like,
how many VMs have external IP

00:35:27.780 --> 00:35:29.820
addresses, or, how
is my persistent disk

00:35:29.820 --> 00:35:32.020
versus local SSD mix
changing over time?

00:35:32.020 --> 00:35:33.870
That's a cost optimization one.

00:35:33.870 --> 00:35:36.990
Or, because this
API also tells you

00:35:36.990 --> 00:35:38.950
about the policies
that are applied,

00:35:38.950 --> 00:35:41.340
you can also say, hey
at a particular moment

00:35:41.340 --> 00:35:44.530
in time, who had access to a
particular set of resources?

00:35:44.530 --> 00:35:46.950
For example, if you
are investigating

00:35:46.950 --> 00:35:49.860
a security incident or
something like that.

00:35:49.860 --> 00:35:53.460
You can imagine with these
APIs and with just a little bit

00:35:53.460 --> 00:35:55.770
of iteration over the
results, you can really

00:35:55.770 --> 00:35:56.910
do some powerful scanning.

00:35:59.560 --> 00:36:03.220
The last section we have here
is around enacting and enforcing

00:36:03.220 --> 00:36:04.250
policies.

00:36:04.250 --> 00:36:06.490
And so this is all
around, now that I

00:36:06.490 --> 00:36:09.160
know the state of
the world, and now

00:36:09.160 --> 00:36:10.990
that I have the
resource hierarchy

00:36:10.990 --> 00:36:13.900
to reason over that in a
more human comprehensible

00:36:13.900 --> 00:36:17.200
sort of way, how can I start
to put up some guardrails?

00:36:17.200 --> 00:36:21.010
How can I start enforcing
that some of the best

00:36:21.010 --> 00:36:23.470
practices that we just talked
about actually be implemented

00:36:23.470 --> 00:36:26.060
by my organization at large?

00:36:26.060 --> 00:36:27.610
And so when I say
the word policies,

00:36:27.610 --> 00:36:30.550
I mean that in the
broadest sense of the term.

00:36:30.550 --> 00:36:34.090
For a city, a policy
might be all buildings

00:36:34.090 --> 00:36:37.240
need to have two fire escapes,
or buildings in this region

00:36:37.240 --> 00:36:39.360
can only be under 20 stories.

00:36:39.360 --> 00:36:41.680
For a Cloud, in the
Cloud world, a policy

00:36:41.680 --> 00:36:43.535
might be for security purposes.

00:36:43.535 --> 00:36:45.160
You might say, hey,
resources shouldn't

00:36:45.160 --> 00:36:46.840
be shared outside
of the organization,

00:36:46.840 --> 00:36:48.257
or this is a set
of people who can

00:36:48.257 --> 00:36:51.130
do x on this set of resources.

00:36:51.130 --> 00:36:53.350
They can also be for
compliance reasons.

00:36:53.350 --> 00:36:56.080
We used the example
before of the organization

00:36:56.080 --> 00:36:57.388
in the European Union.

00:36:57.388 --> 00:36:58.930
They want to have
a policy that says,

00:36:58.930 --> 00:37:01.600
my users shouldn't be
able to stand up resources

00:37:01.600 --> 00:37:04.560
in the outside of the EU.

00:37:04.560 --> 00:37:06.900
Or they can be for cost savings.

00:37:06.900 --> 00:37:10.020
You can say something like,
hey, VMs in a sandbox project,

00:37:10.020 --> 00:37:12.210
they should be shut down
on weekends and holidays

00:37:12.210 --> 00:37:14.705
you should have VMs
underneath a certain size,

00:37:14.705 --> 00:37:15.580
et cetera, et cetera.

00:37:15.580 --> 00:37:18.670
There's a number of
other reasons too.

00:37:18.670 --> 00:37:21.460
Now, when we talk about
enacting and enforcing policies,

00:37:21.460 --> 00:37:23.650
I want to draw a dichotomy here.

00:37:23.650 --> 00:37:26.290
You can enforce policies
before the fact, which

00:37:26.290 --> 00:37:29.530
is to say you can place limits
on activities before they

00:37:29.530 --> 00:37:31.070
happen.

00:37:31.070 --> 00:37:33.550
I might say users are
not able to create

00:37:33.550 --> 00:37:36.890
a disk in a particular
region, and if they try to,

00:37:36.890 --> 00:37:39.210
they'll get an error.

00:37:39.210 --> 00:37:42.180
Or we can enact policies
after the fact as well.

00:37:42.180 --> 00:37:45.360
So we can maybe be a little
more permissive for users

00:37:45.360 --> 00:37:47.112
and allow them to
take a certain action,

00:37:47.112 --> 00:37:48.570
but once they take
an action, we're

00:37:48.570 --> 00:37:50.100
constantly scanning
the environment

00:37:50.100 --> 00:37:52.500
to see whether or not
the action happened,

00:37:52.500 --> 00:37:54.630
to see what things are
happening and taking action

00:37:54.630 --> 00:37:57.448
against violations of policies.

00:37:57.448 --> 00:37:58.990
So at this point
you might ask, well,

00:37:58.990 --> 00:38:00.720
why not only do things
before the fact?

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:04.380
Why am I even taking the risk of
allowing some actions to happen

00:38:04.380 --> 00:38:06.150
and then reacting to it?

00:38:06.150 --> 00:38:09.120
Well for some policies, for
like life or death policies,

00:38:09.120 --> 00:38:11.430
or security policies,
you should certainly

00:38:11.430 --> 00:38:13.050
enforce them before the fact.

00:38:13.050 --> 00:38:13.990
That's for sure.

00:38:13.990 --> 00:38:17.460
But if you're just enforcing
them before the fact,

00:38:17.460 --> 00:38:19.860
how do you know that you're
doing that in the right way?

00:38:19.860 --> 00:38:22.920
And so one reason to enforce
both before and after the fact

00:38:22.920 --> 00:38:24.460
is defense in depth.

00:38:24.460 --> 00:38:27.420
Let's say I don't want to have
any VMs in my organization

00:38:27.420 --> 00:38:29.580
with external IP addresses
because I'm very worried

00:38:29.580 --> 00:38:31.130
about data exploitation.

00:38:31.130 --> 00:38:33.630
What I can do is, I certainly
won't allow my users to create

00:38:33.630 --> 00:38:37.140
VMs with external IPs, but
then I also scan after the fact

00:38:37.140 --> 00:38:40.680
to see what are all my VMs,
and do any have external IPs.

00:38:40.680 --> 00:38:44.040
And so I can be doubly confident
that I truly am following

00:38:44.040 --> 00:38:46.290
and abiding by the policy.

00:38:46.290 --> 00:38:49.430
Another reason to enforce after
the fact is for things where

00:38:49.430 --> 00:38:51.410
they're more of a best
practice, or I more

00:38:51.410 --> 00:38:53.750
want to nudge my users
towards that behavior.

00:38:53.750 --> 00:38:56.330
And in that case, you'll
find that by enforcing

00:38:56.330 --> 00:38:59.960
after the fact, which I'll give
some examples on in a second,

00:38:59.960 --> 00:39:02.330
you can be a lot more
expressive and do

00:39:02.330 --> 00:39:08.000
a lot more actions as opposed
to enforcing before the fact.

00:39:08.000 --> 00:39:12.560
But first we'll zoom in on
enforcement before the fact.

00:39:12.560 --> 00:39:16.310
We have a number of tools to
set policies that will prevent

00:39:16.310 --> 00:39:18.050
behavior before it happens.

00:39:18.050 --> 00:39:20.190
Of course, one is
the IAM policy.

00:39:20.190 --> 00:39:22.910
So this is using the built in
identity and access management

00:39:22.910 --> 00:39:25.550
tools, you can extend
policies on resources

00:39:25.550 --> 00:39:27.600
to say who can do what on them.

00:39:27.600 --> 00:39:29.840
So for example, I can
apply a policy that

00:39:29.840 --> 00:39:32.035
says, this particular
set of users,

00:39:32.035 --> 00:39:33.410
they have this
set of permissions

00:39:33.410 --> 00:39:37.460
on this organization,
so on and so forth.

00:39:40.230 --> 00:39:43.160
We also have the concept of
an organizational policy,

00:39:43.160 --> 00:39:46.340
so whereas IAM policies are
in the business of granting

00:39:46.340 --> 00:39:48.920
permissions to
particular sets of users,

00:39:48.920 --> 00:39:51.180
when you set up an
organization policy,

00:39:51.180 --> 00:39:53.330
these are in the business
of placing restrictions

00:39:53.330 --> 00:39:54.360
on resources.

00:39:54.360 --> 00:39:57.440
So I can set up a organization
policy for example, that says,

00:39:57.440 --> 00:39:59.630
I don't want any
external IP addresses.

00:39:59.630 --> 00:40:01.438
We're also working
on another one

00:40:01.438 --> 00:40:03.230
calling back to the
previous example, which

00:40:03.230 --> 00:40:05.900
says I don't want to
have any resources stored

00:40:05.900 --> 00:40:10.170
in a particular
location or region.

00:40:10.170 --> 00:40:11.730
And finally, another
tool that we're

00:40:11.730 --> 00:40:15.030
seeing becoming a lot more
prevalent among larger

00:40:15.030 --> 00:40:18.180
organizations for
enforcement before the fact,

00:40:18.180 --> 00:40:21.330
is the concept of having
a level of automation

00:40:21.330 --> 00:40:24.690
sitting between the
users and the GCE.

00:40:24.690 --> 00:40:26.820
This is an actual real-life
case study from one

00:40:26.820 --> 00:40:27.540
of our customers.

00:40:27.540 --> 00:40:30.840
What they do is, they don't
allow any of their end users

00:40:30.840 --> 00:40:33.120
to create projects.

00:40:33.120 --> 00:40:35.760
Instead, these users go
to a service now portal

00:40:35.760 --> 00:40:37.710
that they've implemented.

00:40:37.710 --> 00:40:39.240
They go in the
service now portal,

00:40:39.240 --> 00:40:41.580
and they request a
project be created.

00:40:41.580 --> 00:40:44.010
That asks them questions to
the user about what they want

00:40:44.010 --> 00:40:47.100
the project to be used for, and
in turn, it might even kick off

00:40:47.100 --> 00:40:48.570
some approval workflows.

00:40:48.570 --> 00:40:50.580
But after that,
assuming that it's

00:40:50.580 --> 00:40:53.400
approved, what this
service now does

00:40:53.400 --> 00:40:56.400
is it calls up a Jenkins
job, which will automatically

00:40:56.400 --> 00:40:59.562
create a project on
behalf of the user.

00:40:59.562 --> 00:41:01.020
And when it creates
the project, it

00:41:01.020 --> 00:41:03.480
is able to create the project
with some specific set

00:41:03.480 --> 00:41:06.648
of defaults that are kind of
more trusted and more safe.

00:41:06.648 --> 00:41:08.940
So for example, what they do
is they delete the default

00:41:08.940 --> 00:41:11.400
network, because by default
we'll create a new network

00:41:11.400 --> 00:41:12.910
with every single project.

00:41:12.910 --> 00:41:15.570
And instead, they'll hook up
the project to a shared network

00:41:15.570 --> 00:41:18.060
that they've created which is
more centrally administered,

00:41:18.060 --> 00:41:22.110
and also in doing so, they can
set up some other good defaults

00:41:22.110 --> 00:41:24.990
on the project, like the IAM
roles and things like that.

00:41:24.990 --> 00:41:30.030
And in doing so, I think they've
come to a good mix of agility

00:41:30.030 --> 00:41:31.113
with the right guardrails.

00:41:31.113 --> 00:41:33.530
So their developers can still
move quickly, they can still

00:41:33.530 --> 00:41:35.252
create projects as
needed, but now they

00:41:35.252 --> 00:41:36.960
have the right guardrails
on that project

00:41:36.960 --> 00:41:39.750
to make sure developers
don't veer off a cliff.

00:41:42.982 --> 00:41:44.940
So this is all I'll say
on the subject for now,

00:41:44.940 --> 00:41:47.398
but I do want to [? call ?] we
did have a session that goes

00:41:47.398 --> 00:41:50.130
much, much deeper into
all of these topics

00:41:50.130 --> 00:41:53.190
yesterday called Best Practices
for privacy and security

00:41:53.190 --> 00:41:54.180
and Compute Engine.

00:41:54.180 --> 00:41:56.050
If this subject
matter interests you,

00:41:56.050 --> 00:41:57.950
I would highly suggest
you check that out.

00:42:00.530 --> 00:42:03.190
You can also do
enforcement after the fact.

00:42:03.190 --> 00:42:06.010
And this is where some of
the really interesting pieces

00:42:06.010 --> 00:42:07.010
come together.

00:42:07.010 --> 00:42:11.230
So one best practice
we're seeing in the field

00:42:11.230 --> 00:42:15.200
is the ability to take automated
actions from audit logs.

00:42:15.200 --> 00:42:17.710
So as I mentioned before, you
can actually export an audit

00:42:17.710 --> 00:42:19.900
log to Pub/Sub,
and what that means

00:42:19.900 --> 00:42:22.990
is as those actions
happen in real time,

00:42:22.990 --> 00:42:25.900
you'll get a push to Pub/Sub
that you can then subscribe to,

00:42:25.900 --> 00:42:27.730
and then you can
take actions on.

00:42:27.730 --> 00:42:30.130
So at the start of
the talk, I talked

00:42:30.130 --> 00:42:32.950
about schedule snapshots
and the ability

00:42:32.950 --> 00:42:34.273
to set up those schedules.

00:42:34.273 --> 00:42:36.190
Let's say you wanted to
enforce that as a best

00:42:36.190 --> 00:42:37.540
practice in your organization.

00:42:37.540 --> 00:42:39.620
You want to say, anytime
a disk is created,

00:42:39.620 --> 00:42:41.230
I want to encourage
my users, or I

00:42:41.230 --> 00:42:44.900
want to create a schedule
snapshot for those users.

00:42:44.900 --> 00:42:47.130
So what you could
do, for example,

00:42:47.130 --> 00:42:50.920
is you could take your logs and
export them to Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:42:50.920 --> 00:42:52.780
and you might constrain
or filter your logs

00:42:52.780 --> 00:42:59.110
to just the ones that are disk
creation events or VM creation

00:42:59.110 --> 00:43:02.420
events that have disk
creation associated with them.

00:43:02.420 --> 00:43:04.150
So now you have a
Cloud Pub/Sub topic

00:43:04.150 --> 00:43:06.580
that has basically a constant
stream of every disk that's

00:43:06.580 --> 00:43:09.760
being created in
your organization.

00:43:09.760 --> 00:43:11.830
And from there, you can
set up Cloud Functions

00:43:11.830 --> 00:43:14.050
or any other kind
of managed service,

00:43:14.050 --> 00:43:18.150
or it could even be a VM that
just listens to this Pub/Sub

00:43:18.150 --> 00:43:19.390
topic that you set up.

00:43:19.390 --> 00:43:21.820
So basically, it gets triggered
every time a disk gets

00:43:21.820 --> 00:43:23.685
created in your organization.

00:43:23.685 --> 00:43:25.060
And what it can
do is it can read

00:43:25.060 --> 00:43:28.510
that audit log, it can
understand whether or not

00:43:28.510 --> 00:43:32.170
this disk has a schedule
snapshot attached to it,

00:43:32.170 --> 00:43:34.130
and if not, it can take action.

00:43:34.130 --> 00:43:36.938
So it might, for example,
email the creator

00:43:36.938 --> 00:43:38.980
and say, hey, I notice
you didn't have a schedule

00:43:38.980 --> 00:43:39.698
snapshot.

00:43:39.698 --> 00:43:40.990
You should probably attach one.

00:43:40.990 --> 00:43:43.157
Or it could go further and
just automatically create

00:43:43.157 --> 00:43:44.615
a snapshot for you.

00:43:44.615 --> 00:43:46.990
So in this case you're sharing
to guide your organization

00:43:46.990 --> 00:43:49.450
towards a set of best
practices, and you also

00:43:49.450 --> 00:43:52.525
see how this approach has
a lot more flexibility.

00:43:52.525 --> 00:43:55.150
It takes a little bit more work,
but has a lot more flexibility

00:43:55.150 --> 00:43:57.910
and kind of how
heavy handed you want

00:43:57.910 --> 00:44:01.090
to be with your guardrails.

00:44:01.090 --> 00:44:03.680
You can also imagine if you
have an aggregated log export,

00:44:03.680 --> 00:44:04.930
you can make this even easier.

00:44:04.930 --> 00:44:07.480
You can scale this across
your entire organization,

00:44:07.480 --> 00:44:09.568
because now you can
deploy this once

00:44:09.568 --> 00:44:11.860
and it applies to all the
logs that are being generated

00:44:11.860 --> 00:44:15.102
across your organization.

00:44:15.102 --> 00:44:16.560
Another best practice
we're seeing,

00:44:16.560 --> 00:44:20.670
and this is around automated
scanning of your environments,

00:44:20.670 --> 00:44:23.400
using the information
from the automated scan

00:44:23.400 --> 00:44:26.500
to check for [? pilot ?]
policy violations.

00:44:26.500 --> 00:44:28.650
So in the example
here, let's say

00:44:28.650 --> 00:44:32.190
we want to ensure that no disks
exist outside of the European

00:44:32.190 --> 00:44:33.765
Union.

00:44:33.765 --> 00:44:35.640
Then what I can do is
I can use a Cloud asset

00:44:35.640 --> 00:44:39.660
inventory to export all of
the disks in my organization

00:44:39.660 --> 00:44:40.980
to GCS.

00:44:40.980 --> 00:44:43.200
Once I have that list,
it's a pretty simple job

00:44:43.200 --> 00:44:46.930
to just iterate over that file,
detect the region for each disk

00:44:46.930 --> 00:44:49.543
And if there is a disk
outside of the European Union,

00:44:49.543 --> 00:44:50.960
I can take the
appropriate action.

00:44:50.960 --> 00:44:52.950
Maybe that means
I file a ticket,

00:44:52.950 --> 00:44:57.420
or I contact the person who owns
it, or a more extreme version,

00:44:57.420 --> 00:44:59.310
I can just go and
delete that disk.

00:44:59.310 --> 00:45:02.310
And then you can wait some
interval and repeat step one.

00:45:02.310 --> 00:45:05.370
So you can constantly be
scanning your environment,

00:45:05.370 --> 00:45:07.400
and in doing so, it's
also a great vehicle

00:45:07.400 --> 00:45:09.150
to start enforcing
some of these policies.

00:45:12.662 --> 00:45:14.870
So with that, that brings
us to the end of this talk.

00:45:14.870 --> 00:45:16.700
Hopefully this has been
helpful in showing you

00:45:16.700 --> 00:45:18.367
some of the best
practices and the tools

00:45:18.367 --> 00:45:20.680
that we have for
enterprise deployments.

00:45:20.680 --> 00:45:22.560
Thank you very much.

